+/// Persistent, bounded scheduling for the typewriter text animation.
+///
+/// The typewriter effect reveals newly changed glyph output one
+/// character at a time: every changed cell is timestamped one stagger
+/// later than the previous changed cell, in reading order. This
+/// schedule hands those timestamps out and keeps the sequencing alive
+/// across rebuilds so lines written by a shell in quick succession
+/// (one output burst, e.g. a command echo, its error output, and the
+/// next prompt arriving in separate rebuilds) reveal in reading order
+/// instead of all sharing one timestamp and appearing together.
+///
+/// A rebuild starts a fresh burst when there is no schedule yet, when
+/// the previous cascade has fully revealed (the current time is past
+/// the last handed-out timestamp plus the reveal duration), or when
+/// continuing would run the schedule more than `max_ahead` seconds
+/// past the present; the last rule bounds the reveal backlog so a
+/// sustained fast output can never grow the animation window without
+/// limit. A fresh burst starts at the current time, so newly written
+/// lines are never delayed by an already-finished (or unreasonably
+/// long) cascade. Timestamps only ever advance when a changed cell
+/// consumes one, so unchanged content never retimes the schedule.
